    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "okay i get that. but how does the ldl actually do the damage? like what is it actually doing to the artery walls to cause a blockage? is it just sitting there or does it do something specific?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "High levels of LDL particles can infiltrate the inner lining of your arteries, which is called the endothelium. Once there, these LDL particles become oxidized and trigger an inflammatory response. This inflammation attracts other substances and cells that contribute to the buildup of plaque, a hard fatty deposit that narrows and eventually blocks the artery.",
      "source": "target",
